<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="traveling_waves" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Traveling Waves</title>
			
  	<subsubsection>
		<title>Activities</title>
		
		<activity>
			<title>Generating a Wave</title>
			<statement>
				<p>
					
				</p>
				<p>
					A string is tied to a mass on a spring. The mass oscillates 5 times each second, getting as far as <m>8.0 \mathrm{~cm}</m> from equilibrium, generating a wave on the string whose crests are separated by <m>7.2 \mathrm{~m}</m>.  Find the following:
				</p>
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							Wavelength
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Amplitude
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Period
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Frequency
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Wave speed
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Initial phase
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							An equation for the displacement of the string as a function of <m>x</m> and <m>t</m>
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</statement>
			
		</activity>
		<activity>
			<title>Three Waves</title>
			<introduction>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				You create three waves on three identical strings that have the same period <m>T</m>, but they start at a maximum displacement at different times: <m>y_{\text{max}}</m> occurs at <m>t=0</m> for wave 1, at <m>t=T/6</m> for wave 2, and at <m>t=5T/6</m> for wave 3.
			</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Sketch a history graph of each wave (displacement vs. time).
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Sketch a snapshot graph of each wave at <m>t=T/6</m> (displacement vs. position).
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write an equation for each wave.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>
		</activity>

		<activity>
			<title>Leaf on a Lake</title>
			<introduction>
				<p>
					
				</p>
				<p>
					You are watching a leaf bobbing up and down on the surface of a lake (both above and below the surface of the water).  You measure the leaf's highest point above the surface of the water to be <m>12 \mathrm{~cm}</m>.  After <m>3 \mathrm{~s}</m>, the leaf returns to its highest point above the surface of the water. 
				</p>
			</introduction>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Write an equation for the position of the leaf as a function of time.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						What is the leaf's highest speed during its motion?
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Find all the instants in time when the leaf has this speed.
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						How do you think you could use your equation for the position of the leaf to write an equation for the vertical displacement of the water a distance <m>x</m> away from the leaf?  Try writing such an equation!
					</p>
				</statement>
			</task>
			<task>
				<statement>
					<p>
						Start the following <url href="https://beav.es/N7q">simulation</url>.
						</p>
						<ol>
							<li>
								<p>
									Explore each of the three tabs.  Make a list of your observations as you go.
								</p>
							</li>
							<li>
								<p>
									In the water tab, what do you think would happen if you placed a leaf on the surface of the water near the right edge of the tank?  Draw a displacement vs. time graph (like those provided in the simulation) for the leaf.
								</p>
							</li>
							<li>
								<p>
									How does each experiment change if you change the frequency?  Explain briefly.
								</p>
							</li>
							<li>
								<p>
									How does each experiment change if you change the amplitude? Explain briefly. (You may want to turn your sound on.)
								</p>
							</li>
						</ol>
				</statement>
			</task>
		</activity>

	</subsubsection>

		
</section>

